carved into soft stone the meaning of  these symbols lost to us today  but this 5 000 year old chalk drum on  show for the first time gives an  emotional insight into life back then  it was found many miles to the north of  stonehenge in a grave site that was  unearthed in east yorkshire and what  archaeologists found alongside it was  just as interesting they found the  bodies of three children aged between 3  and 12 in a pose like they've never seen  before  the youngest two facing each other  touching hands the eldest placing his or  her arms around them almost cuddling  buried with a clay ball that a child  might play with it's incredibly poignant  and it moves people to tears and it it  shows a sort of vulnerability of the  children and you can only sort of relate  to and sympathize with the community at  the time the parents and the mourners  who buried those children  in the grave  it is the first time such a range of  objects from stonehenge times have been  brought together like this  of course it can't include stonehenge  but it does have the wooden seahenge  found by the shore in norfolk the  british museum setting out to explain  the advanced depths of the neolithic  community's knowledge  a good example of that is is these  beautiful axes on this wall behind us  usually they're stored away but they are  beautiful amazing objects and they're so  key to understanding this period um  which is about the transformation of  land in a sense the creation of the  world we know now the farming world  is an incredible insight into a time  whose people we know were sophisticated  enough to design and build stonehenge  but whose ways remain shrouded in  mystery katie spencer sky news
 

